all the sirens in the world weren't loud  enough to sound the alarm for what was  happening inside tender care living  center  last december within a week of its first  covet case  31 residents were infected another week  later  156 residents and staff were positive  and those numbers would keep careening  out of control  [Music]  james ha could only watch from the  outside praying his 83 year old father  fan kim ha would survive the storm  what are you thinking as you're watching  this on the news i'm thinking  you better be putting in protocols  to protect those who haven't been  infected  tender care did have a plan in place in  case there was an outbreak  it had been in writing since the spring  when the first wave proved how deadly  covet could be inside a long-term care  home  the plan was to use a vacant wing of the  fourth floor to isolate any residents  who were positive  toronto public health says it verified  it and according to the document  tendercare could set up the unit the  same day staff like this personal  support worker  whose identity we've agreed to protect  say they were briefed on the plan  at meetings they told us time and time  again in case of anything  the residents would be moved to the  fourth floor but when covert hit on  december  4th that plan wasn't put into play  and the crowded old home where nearly  all residents share a room  many with three in four people was  vulnerable  what they were doing and i was so upset  they were moving the residents from unit  to unit  it was a big and huge mistake by moving  the residents back and forth  so it kept spreading like that six days  into the outbreak  and a team from scarborough health  network came in to support tender care  pressing them to enact the plan an email  from the home to families the following  day  said we've agreed to move all residents  positive with coveted 19 to our fourth  floor  we have 20 vacant rooms but the fourth  floor  was never used everybody was like  stunned  because the residents weren't moving to  the fourth floor and what did they say  to you what was the answer  i think they weren't prepared they were  just talking but there was nothing being  done  the company contracted to manage tender  care extendicare  one of the largest owners and operators  of long-term care homes in canada  said staff were falling ill and they  didn't have the resources to open the  floor  it was now 12 days into the outbreak and  there were warnings  if this continued everyone could become  infected  toronto public health intervened  ordering the home to separate covet  positive and negative residents  but by this point a hundred and eighteen  residents  already had coveted i think it was way  too slow  so far james has father had not been  infected  toronto health i believe should have  acted  much more aggressively as soon as they  are aware of the outbreak  toronto public health says the next day  the home provided a new plan for  separating residents  and began implementing it right away but  two of the home's four doctors were sick  and staffing levels were plummeting a  call went out among a group of doctors  on social media  that tender care needed help dr kate  greenway  a family physician put her hand up when  i arrived on sunday  and i started seeing these people that  were very ill certainly what i was  ordering immediately was oxygen and  dexamethasone  and then at some point later in that  that shift you know one of the nurses  just said well we don't have any more  anywhere what oxygen they were out of  oxygen right  i sort of said back i don't i don't  understand like what  where do we get the oxygen and they said  well we have to order it from the oxygen  company but then they have to deliver it  and that takes quite a lot of time  so without oxygen and without  dexamethasone to open up those lungs  yeah no you're in trouble tendercare  says it tried to secure supplies  but the region was seeing a surge in  covet cases and resources were scarce  to green away the home felt frenetic  it was pretty shocking to me that it was  possible  to seem so unprepared for for an  outbreak greenway was able to get oxygen  and dexamethasone from scarborough  hospital  you personally made the call right he  didn't know who was insurance i did not  know  tendicare says its executive director  francis martus was in charge  until he too contracted covid we reached  him by phone  we're just trying to figure out why the  fourth floor wasn't used because  could you be willing to do an on-camera  interview with us  tender care says after martis was  infected directors from extendicare took  charge  but adds decisions on how to separate  positive and negative cases  were guided by toronto public health  management did tell staff that if they  felt sick they shouldn't come to work  and they were screened at the door but  at least one employee we spoke with said  she worked through her shift while her  fever grew because she had no sick days  left  tendercare says it offered unlimited  paid sick days for kovid  but multiple workers told global news  they knew nothing of this policy  if you don't get sick days you have no  sick days in the bank you won't get paid  management didn't say to you if you're  sick we'll still pay you to stay home  no they're not going to pay you to stay  home  on christmas day 22 days after its first  case tender care relinquished management  of its home  to north york general hospital dr kevin  katz is the hospital's director of  infection prevention and control  it was it was widespread and the  transmission was rampant at the time  that we stepped in right on christmas  day  stabilizing the home became the focus  for katz's team  and they recruited 50 doctors from  across the province  was tender care ready for the second  wave well clearly not  i believe every long-term care home was  asked to fill out a preparedness  survey and they were all told formally  that they were accountable for infection  prevention and control but  clearly they weren't ready to manage  what they experienced  this is the preparedness assessment  tender care filled out in august  months before the outbreak it ranked  itself 4 out of 5 near the top of the  scale for readiness  in every category including infection  prevention and control  stating audits are done monthly and  training is provided to all staff  but put to the test those on the ground  said  there were gaps i think it's clear  that the the issues when we went in  uh related to training and education  how they were using ppe environmental  cleaning it's a repeated theme  in all of these very very large  outbreaks ministry inspectors had been  flagging issues at tender care for years  including at the beginning of the  outbreak when they documented  widespread problems with infection  prevention and control  and improper use of ppe tendercare told  global news it takes swift action to fix  issues raised in ministry inspections  they should have just declared  this this disaster zone and moved in as  soon as their inspectors  sent in their report it was early  january  fully a month into the outbreak when  james ha  got the news he had been dreading my  worst nightmare came to reality when  i learned to the beginning of january  that he was infected  his father was transferred to hospital  where he would spend three weeks before  returning to tender care  a much more frail version of himself  covet had ravaged his body and coupled  with dementia  robbed him of his ability to swallow and  then  yesterday  sorry he had some food particles  that came into his lungs and he died he  died  basically of suffocation fan  kim ha isn't included in the staggering  death toll at tender care  but his son has little doubt had he not  contracted covid  he would still be here he would  absolutely still be with us today  [Music]  the owners of tender care during the  outbreak didn't respond or refused to do  an interview with us i'm trying to reach  john is he around the president john  doherty  i will give him the card and if he wants  to speak to you he will deferred our  questions to the management company  extendicare its ceo  michael guerrero we're trying to reach  dr grayer we have for quite some time  wouldn't come downstairs  to speak with us my husband's probably  on the phone  got it can i leave you my card in a  joint email response  tender care and extendicare said they  responded immediately  and through every available channel to  get the supports needed to the home  but the stress on the health system in  the region at the time  meant that delivery of supplies  additional staff and other support  was not immediate on february 1st  the outbreak at tender care living  center was declared over  81 people were dead all but five  residents in the home of just over 200  people  were infected and 105 staff  tested positive some continue to  experience symptoms today  other workers took cove at home to their  families infecting them as well  and among the unseen scars of covid the  trauma  of putting body after body into bag for  some workers to stay away  even longer it's essential that we  talk about what we saw because we want  to prevent this happening in any other  home  there is deep sorrow among those who  stepped foot inside tender care during  those  harrowing eight weeks and a growing  sense of frustration  that we should have done better there  are major gaps  very very serious gaps in the system  that are letting these people down  and some of us went in to try to fill  those gaps  this wasn't a gap that we could fill  there was a systemic failure  somewhere in the system that allowed it  to come to the levels  it had come to when we walked in
 

